content.theory:
Motion makes interfaces feel alive. CSS has two tools: **transitions** (smooth changes between states) and **keyframe animations** (multi-step motion).

### Transitions — smooth a property change
```css
.button {
  background: #4f8f57;
  transition: background 0.3s ease, transform 0.2s ease;
}
.button:hover {
  background: #3a7342;
  transform: scale(1.05);   /* grows slightly */
}
```
- `transition: [property] [duration] [timing] [delay]`
- Only the **listed properties** animate
- Triggered by a state change (hover, focus, class toggles)

### Transforms
```css
.element {
  transform: rotate(45deg);
  transform: translateX(20px);   /* move */
  transform: scale(1.5);          /* grow */
}
```
`transform` doesn't reflow the layout — it's cheap and smooth.

### Keyframes — multi-step animation
```css
@keyframes bounce {
  0%   { transform: translateY(0); }
  50%  { transform: translateY(-20px); }
  100% { transform: translateY(0); }
}

.badge {
  animation: bounce 1s ease-in-out infinite;
}
```

### Animation shorthand
```css
animation: name duration timing delay iteration-count;
animation: bounce 1s ease 0s infinite;
```

### Accessibility
Respect users who prefer reduced motion:
```css
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  .badge { animation: none; }
}
```

Rule of thumb: use transitions for hover/press feedback, keyframes for ongoing attention-grabbing motion — and keep it subtle.

